STEP 1: Delete and rebuild the cluster from memory
--------------------------------------------------
kind delete cluster --name lab
kind get clusters            # should print nothing / "No kind clusters found."
docker ps                    # node containers are gone

cd ~/k8s-journey/kind
Try to type kind-config.yaml yourself first (peek only if stuck), then:
  kind create cluster --name lab --config kind-config.yaml
  k get nodes


STEP 2: Install k9s (terminal UI)
---------------------------------
cd /tmp
curl -LO https://github.com/derailed/k9s/releases/latest/download/k9s_Linux_amd64.tar.gz
tar -xzf k9s_Linux_amd64.tar.gz k9s
sudo install -m 0755 k9s /usr/local/bin/k9s
k9s version
k9s

Navigation inside k9s:
  :pods      view pods          :nodes   view nodes
  :ns        namespaces         /text    filter
  d          describe           l        logs
  s          shell              ctrl-d   delete
  0          all namespaces     :q       quit

Spend 15 minutes exploring kube-system with it.


STEP 3: Install kubectx and kubens
----------------------------------
sudo git clone https://github.com/ahmetb/kubectx /opt/kubectx
sudo ln -s /opt/kubectx/kubectx /usr/local/bin/kubectx
sudo ln -s /opt/kubectx/kubens  /usr/local/bin/kubens
kubectx            # lists contexts, current is highlighted
kubens             # lists namespaces


STEP 4: Run your own image in Kubernetes
----------------------------------------
Use the image you pushed on Day 3:

k run myapp --image=YOURUSER/myapp:v1 --port=5000
k get pod myapp -o wide
k port-forward pod/myapp 5000:5000

Terminal 2:
  curl localhost:5000

You should see "Hello from v1! Served by container: myapp".
The hostname is now the POD name. This closes the loop: you built an
image (Day 3), pushed it to a registry, and Kubernetes pulled and ran it.


STEP 5: Break/fix drills
------------------------
Rule: fix each one using ONLY describe, logs, and get events.

Drill 1: wrong image tag
  k run d1 --image=nginx:nope

Drill 2: a command that exits immediately
  k run d2 --image=busybox -- sh -c "echo starting; exit 1"

Drill 3: a pod that asks for impossible memory
  k run d3 --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml > d3.yaml
  Edit d3.yaml. Under the container (same indent as "image:") add:
      resources:
        requests:
          memory: "100Gi"
  k apply -f d3.yaml

Now investigate:
  k get pods
  k describe pod d1 | tail -15
  k logs d2
  k logs d2 --previous
  k describe pod d3 | tail -10

Fill in this table in your notes:

  Pod  Status you saw       Reason in describe/logs        Fix
  ---  -------------------  -----------------------------  ----------
  d1   ImagePullBackOff     ...                            ...
  d2   CrashLoopBackOff     ...                            ...
  d3   Pending              0/3 nodes ... Insufficient     ...
                            memory

Clean up:
  k delete pod d1 d2 d3 myapp


